  "title": "CHAPTER 4: RBI REGULATIONS AND BANKING NORMS",
  "order": 4,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "2a8p6iz",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4.1 Basel Norms"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Basel III capital adequacy norms require Indian commercial banks to maintain a minimum Capital to Risk-Weighted Assets Ratio (CRAR) of 11.5%.",
        "Domestic Scheduled Commercial Banks are mandated to allocate 40% of ANBC to Priority Sector Lending, whereas RRBs and SFBs must allocate 75%.",
        "An asset becomes a Non-Performing Asset (NPA) if interest or principal installment remains overdue for more than 90 days.",
        "The SARFAESI Act, 2002 empowers banks to repossess and auction secured assets without judicial intervention for loan recovery.",
        "Financial Inclusion initiatives like PMJDY aim to provide unbanked households access to basic savings accounts, credit, and insurance."
      ],
      "id": "99u913c"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Domestic PSL Target",
          "value": "40%",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "NPA Overdue Threshold",
          "value": "90 Days",
          "icon": "clock"
        },
        {
          "label": "SARFAESI Act Enactment",
          "value": "2002",
